00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.070
I know watch it later.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:34.060
So, first question.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.270
This one's from our
developer community.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:38.550
And the question is, what
can managers and leaders

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:41.500
do to help their teams adopt
modern development workflows

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:42.000
and tools?

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.670
I know that this is a question
we get a lot as advocates when

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:45.930
we're out in the field.

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:47.770
People want to know,
where do I start?

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:48.430
What can I do?

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:51.760
How do I make this
move for my team?

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:53.210
URS HOLZLE: Great question.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:57.410
And actually, much easier
asked than answered.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.950
So there's no magic
bullet in the end.

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:05.650
I think the key, I
would say, is to try

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:07.010
to learn from someone else.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:09.550
And in a way, that's what
we're trying to do with Anthos.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:13.090
So you can look at Anthos
as a technology stack,

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:17.320
but it's just as much a
set of practices that have

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:19.030
proven to work well over time.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:21.130
And so when you
use Anthos, you're

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:23.570
really introduced
to those techniques,

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:26.350
be that about the
development self,

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:29.710
or how you push,
do gradual pushes,

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:32.350
or actually, how you
operate or how you secure.

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:35.950
So I think the key
thing is that you

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:40.780
want to pick something where
actually, the workflow itself

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:44.460
is opinionated, someone's
actually thought about it,

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.930
and where you buy not just
a piece of technology,

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:49.300
or you don't just use
a number of tools,

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:53.272
but you really use it together
in sort of an end to end way,

00:11:53.272 --> 00:11:54.730
and you try to
learn from the best.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:58.070
There's a ton of content
on YouTube and so on,

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:02.920
and there's a ton of content
on our developer websites.

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:08.273
And that's actually what Aja
and her team are great for.

00:12:08.273 --> 00:12:09.940
AJA HAMMERLY: Oh, I'm
glad you think so.

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:14.020
So you're saying that Anthos
is both technology and best

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.270
practices that have been
tried and true by people

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:19.242
who've done this a lot,
like Google, but others.

00:12:19.242 --> 00:12:21.700
And one of those best practices
that I've heard a lot about

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:22.355
is multi-cloud.

00:12:22.355 --> 00:12:23.980
It's one of the main
things that people

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:26.120
are excited about that
I talk to with Anthos,

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:28.508
although there's lots of
cool things in Anthos.

00:12:28.508 --> 00:12:30.050
Can you talk a bit
about multi-cloud?

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:32.590
Like, what are some of the
trade-offs versus benefits

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:35.020
of going with a multi cloud
strategy versus a single cloud

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:35.620
strategy?

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:37.703
I know this is something
a lot of bigger companies

00:12:37.703 --> 00:12:39.870
are thinking about right now.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.940
URS HOLZLE: Yeah,
I think, actually

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:47.050
that is a much easier question,
because the whole premise--

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:51.060
like, the trade-off
usually comes in two forms.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.830
One, it says, well, the upside
of multi cloud obviously

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:57.155
is that if you pick something,
are you really going

00:12:57.155 --> 00:13:00.140
to be sure that for
the next 20 years,

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:03.670
this is the right vendor to pick
until you want to have options.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:07.670
And, also, you want to
use the best of any world

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:12.130
and move at your own pace,
leave some things on premise

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.200
because right now you
don't want to deal with it.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:16.250
Move them at your own time.

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:18.850
But then the downside, or
the portrayed downside,

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:23.050
is that, well, let's say,
if you use three clouds,

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:25.840
you actually need to train
your team three times.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:28.070
And you do security in
three different ways.

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:30.980
And you do all kinds of
things three different ways.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.160
Plus, if you want to
be common between them,

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:37.390
you're really dumbing
down what your cloud is.

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:41.350
And you're sort of giving up
most of the advantage of cloud.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:44.510
Now fortunately, that
is actually false.

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:49.660
And Anthos actually shows why
because the beauty of Anthos

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:53.980
is that it doesn't
try to do too much.

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:56.710
It standardizes the things
that should not be different,

00:13:56.710 --> 00:14:01.580
the things that aren't
necessarily special,

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:05.500
let's say, how do you
actually release a binary,

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:09.160
how do you configure something,
how do you define a load

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.580
balancer, how do you distribute
certificates to your services.

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.100
These are all things you
have to do every day.

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:18.760
And they should not be
different between the clouds.

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:22.000
And so Anthos says, learn
it once with Anthos.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:25.030
And then you can pick your
cloud or multiple clouds

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:26.260
or stay on premise.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:28.720
And you don't have to
learn that task again.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:32.230
So for all the tasks and
practices and workflows

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:36.670
that Anthos covers, you really
have just one team, one time

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:38.020
where you have to learn it.

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:40.710
And then you can still
take the advantage

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:43.240
of the uniqueness
of each environment

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.210
without having to relearn
all the basics again.

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:50.770
So fortunately, I think,
this is a false trade-off.

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:54.280
And, I think, you really want
to pick a platform that does not

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.980
lock you in for
the next 20 years

00:14:56.980 --> 00:15:00.800
into one way of doing things.

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.670
So in my view, that's an
easy answer, pure win.

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:06.070
Go with multi cloud, or
be ready for multi cloud

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:07.930
and have the option,
because who knows

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:11.265
what's going to happen
in the next 10, 20 years.

00:15:11.265 --> 00:15:12.890
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah,
so it sounds like--

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:15.110
this was my understanding,
that multi cloud

00:15:15.110 --> 00:15:17.270
has a lot of advantages,
flexibility, being

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:19.730
able to do things on the
right timeline for your team,

00:15:19.730 --> 00:15:23.470
being able to use cloud-specific
features that you need.

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:26.420
But Anthos is kind of
the secret sauce here

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.420
in that it takes away a
lot of the disadvantages

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:30.545
about having to learn
multiple clouds, about having

00:15:30.545 --> 00:15:32.350
to have a team trained
on multiple clouds.

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:32.850
And that's--

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:33.110
URS HOLZLE: Exactly said.

00:15:33.110 --> 00:15:34.110
AJA HAMMERLY: --awesome.

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.650
I know that I've had to make
a decision about on prem

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:37.522
or cloud or both.

00:15:37.522 --> 00:15:39.230
And both was always
scary just because of

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:42.570
the sheer information overload
that we had to keep track of.

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:44.330
I'm glad Anthos exists now.

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:46.160
We didn't have it back then.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:48.830
So now we got a
question from Twitter.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:51.890
According to you, what are the
missing pieces of cloud today?

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:57.350
URS HOLZLE: I'd say lots.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:15:59.690
And actually let me put it
in a slightly different way.

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:05.990
I think, if we put ourselves
in our mind into 2025

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:11.450
and we look back on 2020
and the cloud of 2020,

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.970
I think, we'll be
embarrassed that we liked it

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:18.500
and that we thought it was good
because it will seem incredibly

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:24.300
primitive because even
though cloud is 10 years old,

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:27.030
it actually still is
in its early stages.

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:29.510
So I often compare it
to kind of mobile phones

00:16:29.510 --> 00:16:31.550
after the first 10, 15 years.

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:33.920
It was sort of Nokia
and BlackBerry, right?

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:36.410
It wasn't Android and iOS.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:39.510
And so the Android and iOS
of cloud is still coming.

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:40.910
And one of the
things that really

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:44.030
made that explosion
of functionality

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:48.230
in the mobile space, that made
you really rethink what you

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.050
expect of a mobile
phone, that made

00:16:50.050 --> 00:16:54.200
you look back on your
feature phone and kind of

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:56.630
be surprised that
you ever liked it,

00:16:56.630 --> 00:17:01.280
was the fact that there
were software platforms,

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:04.050
a set of APIs that
were auto update

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:08.420
and that allow you to roll
out your service to anyone

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.910
on that platform and not
have a ton of friction

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:14.190
from version matrix
and sort of anything.

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:16.579
And so that's one of the things
that I think really today

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:18.410
is missing in a cloud.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:21.950
If you're on a single
cloud, then things are fine.

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:23.510
In a sense, they're consistent.

00:17:23.510 --> 00:17:28.130
But be aware if you ever see
the need to either remain

00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:30.088
on premise or use
another cloud because it

00:17:30.088 --> 00:17:32.630
is an awesome service because
you're just completely starting

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:33.330
from scratch.

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:35.820
And I think five years from
now, you'll just say, wow,

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:39.110
I cannot believe that this
was missing back then.

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.150
It's kind of like saying,
what there were phones

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:42.320
without an app store on it?

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.270
That is really hard to believe.

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:47.240
You mean a phone that
was there to make calls?

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:48.760
That's really all it did?

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.490
It's hard for us to
think about today.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.720
So, I think, I predict
that in 2025 we'll

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:56.780
really be in that point where
we kind of are embarrassed

00:17:56.780 --> 00:17:59.980
about the cloud of 2020
because from the developers

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:02.100
perspective, from the
security perspective,

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:04.250
from the operations
perspective, and really

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:08.350
from the portability
perspective, today's cloud

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:10.710
and, actually, today's
cloud world really

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:12.160
isn't a great world.

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:15.403
And we want to change that.

00:18:15.403 --> 00:18:16.320
AJA HAMMERLY: Awesome.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:17.980
And the phone analogy
was super helpful.

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:19.440
I know that I
explained to my nieces

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.482
a couple months ago that
my first BlackBerry only

00:18:21.482 --> 00:18:22.380
had the game Snake.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.900
And they were horrified,
horrified that I didn't

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:26.323
have an app store to use.

00:18:26.323 --> 00:18:28.240
So I'm curious what
that's going to look like.

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:30.073
It's going to be amazing
for the cloud world

00:18:30.073 --> 00:18:33.280
to have that big of a
transformation and so quickly.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:35.312
So let's take another
question from Midori.

00:18:35.312 --> 00:18:37.520
Actually, this question's
from a developer community.

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:40.660
And this one's close to
my heart as a manager.

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:43.020
How do you effectively manage
high-performing technical

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:43.520
teams?

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:45.060
My team is awesome,
and any tips you

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:47.310
can give for the managers
in the audience about how we

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:50.770
can do better would be great.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:51.540
URS HOLZLE: Wow.

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:51.840
OK.

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.382
That's a complicated
question, again.

00:18:53.382 --> 00:18:54.570
So there's no simple answer.

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:58.860
I think, the one thing I
would always mention first

00:18:58.860 --> 00:19:02.340
that people really
underappreciate is hiring,

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:08.370
because if you hire the
right people, then A, it's

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:10.410
going to make a bigger
difference than anything

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:13.620
else you do, and B,
management actually

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:18.450
becomes much easier because
you have great people who

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:19.750
are doing a great job.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:21.300
And they need your support.

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.870
But they don't really need
your sort of corrective action

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:25.750
all the time.

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:30.450
And let me just double down
on the importance of hiring.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:34.200
If you think about it, suppose
that you're at your company

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:37.200
and suppose you spend
a full 10% of your time

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.180
for an entire year--

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:43.790
so 10% every single week
you spend on hiring.

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:45.030
You've interviewed people.

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:47.310
You're trying to recruit
people, whatever.

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:51.750
And suppose for all that effort
you find just one person,

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:57.800
a single person in that year,
who is as good as you are,

00:19:57.800 --> 00:20:00.660
I would argue that with
that 10% of your time

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:03.990
you have doubled your
productivity to the company.

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:07.800
And there's nothing else you
can do in 10% of your time

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:10.200
that doubles your impact.

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:11.460
There's really nothing else.

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:12.480
You cannot work harder.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.700
You can't make better tools.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:16.750
It just does not give
you a factor of 10.

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:19.765
So hiring really, I think,
is the key to management.

00:20:19.765 --> 00:20:21.390
And I think if you
pay attention to it,

00:20:21.390 --> 00:20:26.543
if you do that well, then the
rest follows much more easily.

00:20:26.543 --> 00:20:27.460
AJA HAMMERLY: Awesome.

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:29.610
That's a really
insightful answer.

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:32.580
And I'll have to think
a bit more about that.

00:20:32.580 --> 00:20:35.190
So let's take a more nerdy
question from Midori.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:37.710
Operational frictions
often times leads

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:39.210
to learning opportunities.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:41.970
Eliminating friction equals
eliminating opportunities

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:43.003
for people to learn.

00:20:43.003 --> 00:20:44.670
I think what this
question is saying, is

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.200
that when stuff goes wrong
and your pager goes off,

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:49.670
it is just another
learning opportunity.

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:52.200
Is that always good?

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:53.550
URS HOLZLE: That is right.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:55.230
So, I mean, there's
a good observation

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:57.280
that I separated in two ways.

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:03.240
One is, in the average company,
there is so much productive

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:07.290
learning, learning that
you want to do because you

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:08.760
get advantages from it.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:10.720
And it's sort of pure upside.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:14.020
You learn about containers
about CI/CD or whatever.

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:15.480
In the average
company they're so

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:16.980
much in need of
that that you really

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:21.330
want to focus on that kind
of learning, versus learning

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.180
from your mistakes, so to
speak, or from outages.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:26.340
Now, the second part
is that, actually, it

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.220
is very important to
learn from your outages

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:30.790
or to learn from your mistakes.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:33.900
So in the case of
outages, postmortems,

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:35.490
and especially
blameless postmortems,

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:38.730
they're focusing
on what went wrong

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:40.620
and how do we prevent
that from happening,

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:42.810
not who made a mistake.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:46.980
So blameless postmortems and
learning from mistakes, again,

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:48.780
like maybe a postmortem
for something

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:50.940
in your project or a
technical decision,

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:52.770
that's really when
the learning happens.

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:55.410
So I agree with you
that the best way

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:59.190
to summarize that is let
no crisis go to waste.

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:02.640
A crisis is a huge
opportunity to actually change

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:06.780
how you're doing things
and avoid the next crisis.

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:08.560
So I'm definitely a big fan.

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:11.440
But I think if that becomes the
dominant form of your learning,

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:14.648
then you're in a
problematic spot.

00:22:14.648 --> 00:22:15.440
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:18.020
No, you don't want everything
to be on fire all the time.

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:19.100
And I'm really
glad you mentioned

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:20.060
blameless postmortems.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:22.070
More than anything I've
learned in my five years

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:25.220
at Google, learning about the
blameless postmortem culture

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:28.160
and how you can learn as a team
and do better as a team has

00:22:28.160 --> 00:22:30.350
done wonders, not
only for my work life,

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:32.240
but also for my personal
life and dealing

00:22:32.240 --> 00:22:36.080
with stuff that goes sideways
at home, especially right now.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:37.880
So yeah, that's great.

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:40.130
So now a question from Twitter.

00:22:40.130 --> 00:22:42.800
Who are your inspirations
when it comes to tech?

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:43.730
Who do you look up to?

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:51.110
URS HOLZLE: Boy, I don't want
to name my favorite children, so

00:22:51.110 --> 00:22:52.640
to speak.

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:55.100
But I think what
I admire and what

00:22:55.100 --> 00:23:01.550
inspires me is when there are
things that in retrospect seem

00:23:01.550 --> 00:23:06.180
obvious, but nobody thought
of them for 10 years.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:11.000
So if someone finds
something that five,

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:14.120
even two years from now, if
someone hears about is like,

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:17.430
yeah, that seems pretty obvious
that it's done this way,

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:21.185
but it took 10 years,
20 years to find that.

00:23:21.185 --> 00:23:27.830
That to me is the most
amazing thing and the thing

00:23:27.830 --> 00:23:29.430
I admire most in people.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:32.720
So it's really
often the simplicity

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:36.020
that actually makes
something awesome,

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:40.040
versus something
incredibly complicated.

00:23:40.040 --> 00:23:41.840
That sometimes is unavoidable.

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:46.860
But really what I admire are
things that seem obvious,

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:51.470
but until they became
obvious they weren't obvious.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:52.470
AJA HAMMERLY: All right.

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:53.380
That makes sense.

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:56.645
And that's definitely
been my experience.

00:23:56.645 --> 00:23:58.270
I got some advice
early on in my career

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:01.300
that you should always be
cautious of the person who

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:05.320
asks the seemingly dumb question
because they've likely seen how

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:07.150
simpler your solution could be.

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:08.600
So that's great.

00:24:08.600 --> 00:24:11.920
So our next question is
from, I believe, Midori,

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:13.590
and it is kind of long.

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:14.590
So I'm going to read it.

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:16.875
But then I'm going to try
to summarize it a bit.

00:24:16.875 --> 00:24:18.250
So the question
says, I'm worried

00:24:18.250 --> 00:24:21.070
that the Cloud Run/kNative
for scale to zero

00:24:21.070 --> 00:24:23.830
is leaving customers with
cascading long startup latency

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:26.140
issues as they move to
micro services, which

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:28.975
get paged or swapped
out while not in use.

00:24:28.975 --> 00:24:30.850
Can you endorse a "scale
to one alternative,"

00:24:30.850 --> 00:24:34.900
please, to support always
having an instance ready?

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:37.600
So this, actually, seems
slightly like a feature request

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:38.990
less than a question.

00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:41.740
But it does bring up an
interesting question about,

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:44.440
how do you prioritize the
advantages of something

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:46.630
like scale to zero and
adopting a micro services

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:49.960
architecture over the fact
that there are some downsides?

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:52.780
Scale to zero has
startup latency issues.

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:55.390
How would you address that
if you were trying work

00:24:55.390 --> 00:24:57.670
with a team that was
migrating to a micro services

00:24:57.670 --> 00:25:00.940
architecture on, say, kNative?

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:02.330
URS HOLZLE: Great question.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:07.360
So first of all, not my job
to endorse something or not.

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:10.670
I'm not a sports star that
gets paid for product placement

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:12.440
so to speak.

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:16.720
So I would actually answer
it slightly differently.

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:22.450
And it's not the job of
the provider to pick.

00:25:22.450 --> 00:25:26.620
It's really the job of
each of the teams to pick.

00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:30.790
And I always say, engineering
is the art of the compromise,

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:35.800
because if there was a
dominant, best answer, then

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:37.750
it would be pretty
boring and pretty easy

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:40.360
because everyone does
that, end of story.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:42.760
And so it's really
about trade-offs.

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:46.960
And the trade-offs differ by
industry, by customer, by team

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:47.800
even.

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:50.470
And so in this particular
case, the nice thing

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:54.490
about scale to zero is zero
cost when you don't use it.

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:57.790
And so for some things that's
a really great solution.

00:25:57.790 --> 00:25:59.692
Suppose you have
an internal app,

00:25:59.692 --> 00:26:04.540
and it gives you the
W-2 that you download.

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:06.490
For those of you
international, W-2

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:10.075
is your tax form, your
end-of-year tax form.

00:26:10.075 --> 00:26:14.695
Well, probably 360 days of the
year, nobody uses that app.

00:26:14.695 --> 00:26:16.070
And you don't want
to pay for it.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:17.260
So scale to zero is great.

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:20.800
And if the first person
has to wait a second or two

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:23.170
to download their
tax return, that's

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:26.020
fine, because once
tax season is there,

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:28.120
the application is probably hot.

00:26:28.120 --> 00:26:30.460
On the other hand, as
you say, that actually

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:32.620
if you have a different
app that maybe

00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:35.260
is idle for a little
while but then is

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:38.170
being used again and then idle
for a while and used again,

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:41.260
we kind of don't want to
introduce multi-second startup

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:44.110
latency for the poor person
who is the next person

00:26:44.110 --> 00:26:46.300
to use your app.

00:26:46.300 --> 00:26:47.560
So to me that's a trade-off.

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:49.330
I think it's a good
feature request.

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:51.100
You can actually
easily implement

00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:53.902
that yourself by just
having a little--

00:26:53.902 --> 00:26:56.110
actually you should probably
do end-to-end monitoring

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:57.410
of your key workflows.

00:26:57.410 --> 00:26:59.800
And so if you did that
frequently enough,

00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:03.260
you will keep your functions
warm and your startup latencies

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:03.760
low.

00:27:03.760 --> 00:27:06.220
But to me, again,
it really comes down

00:27:06.220 --> 00:27:10.510
to engineering and figuring
out [INAUDIBLE] the business

00:27:10.510 --> 00:27:12.640
value, what problem
are we solving

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:14.680
and what are the
options that we have

00:27:14.680 --> 00:27:18.100
and among these, which
one is the least worst,

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:19.810
sometimes called the best.

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:22.990
Usually the best isn't
actually flawless.

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:24.860
Everything has some problems.

00:27:24.860 --> 00:27:31.810
So that, I think, is a great
place to end and really say,

00:27:31.810 --> 00:27:32.800
I agree with you.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:35.980
It should be, actually, a simple
configuration option, never

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:36.850
going down to zero.

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:40.907
I value latency over
totally minimal cost.

00:27:40.907 --> 00:27:42.490
I don't think we
have that in kNative.

00:27:42.490 --> 00:27:45.880
But if you know how to
file a feature request,

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:47.758
I think it's a
good one, actually.

00:27:47.758 --> 00:27:48.550
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:50.620
So it seems like the
answer is, like the answer

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:53.090
to almost every technical
question, is it depends.

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:54.700
It depends on your use case.

00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:58.030
And it depends on what the right
choice for your engineering

00:27:58.030 --> 00:28:00.430
team is, which might have to
do with the people you have

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:02.430
and the time they have
and the skills they have.

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:03.950
So that's amazing.

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:06.220
And I know that there's--

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:07.690
I don't know that
we don't have it.

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:08.950
I should know the
answer to this.

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:09.450
And I don't.

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:12.100
And I'm slightly
embarrassed about that.

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:14.500
So next question from Twitter--

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:16.960
actually, no, this next
question is from Midori.

00:28:16.960 --> 00:28:18.850
Ooh, this is a good one.

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:22.180
What are some of your favorite
failed Google infrastructure

00:28:22.180 --> 00:28:24.550
projects that were
such cool ideas

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:29.387
that they had to be tried
irrespective of the outcome?

00:28:29.387 --> 00:28:31.720
URS HOLZLE: Well, first of
all, if you knew the outcome,

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:35.150
you wouldn't have tried it,
probably even if it was cool.

00:28:35.150 --> 00:28:37.538
So, I mean, failures happen
because you don't quite

00:28:37.538 --> 00:28:38.830
predict what's going to happen.

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:41.337
I think, on an
infrastructure side,

00:28:41.337 --> 00:28:43.420
one of the things I would
want to mention actually

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:45.920
goes back 15 years ago.

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:50.470
So 15 years ago we got into
building our own data centers

00:28:50.470 --> 00:28:53.880
because we were sort of running
out of rented data centers.

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:56.470
And we could see the time that
the rents would really go up

00:28:56.470 --> 00:28:58.713
because up until that point--

00:28:58.713 --> 00:29:00.880
for those of you who were
there, the internet bubble

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:02.110
burst in 2000.

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:04.480
And there was just
boatloads of data center

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:07.280
space that was empty
and was cheap to rent.

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:09.040
And so as we were
growing, we were really

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:11.830
growing into a
lot of empty space

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:15.760
that went through bankruptcies
and therefore was really cheap.

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:18.020
So as we were seeing
that coming to an end,

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:19.840
we thought about,
well, what do we

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:22.060
do to make our own data centers?

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:24.220
And, obviously, they've
got to be cheap.

00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:26.740
And not just cheap to build,
but also cheap to operate.

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:30.460
And one of the things we
noticed was that, almost half

00:29:30.460 --> 00:29:34.630
of our data center
bill was energy, power.

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:36.430
And what was actually
really offensive

00:29:36.430 --> 00:29:40.780
is that a significant fraction
of that, again, about half

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:42.970
of that was completely wasted.

00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:47.740
It wasn't actually energy to
power your CPU and your memory.

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:48.970
It just went elsewhere.

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:52.090
The building cooling,
your power supply,

00:29:52.090 --> 00:29:54.590
all kinds of places that you
didn't really care about.

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:57.760
And so we started to build
more efficient data centers.

00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:00.640
And our first
approach was what's

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:03.320
called a containerized
data center.

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:06.125
And so, basically, the idea
was instead of building really

00:30:06.125 --> 00:30:08.500
a building and trying to cool
the building, why don't you

00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:10.675
just cool the servers?

00:30:10.675 --> 00:30:12.050
And by the way,
put those servers

00:30:12.050 --> 00:30:14.270
into literally a
shipping container.

00:30:14.270 --> 00:30:15.980
And then inside that
shipping container,

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:18.220
it's very easy to
control the airflow.

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.390
And then you have sort
of a modular unit,

00:30:20.390 --> 00:30:22.490
where you can deploy,
let's say, space

00:30:22.490 --> 00:30:24.530
for 1,000 servers at a time.

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:26.120
And you just put
this in a warehouse.

00:30:26.120 --> 00:30:27.840
And you can go to YouTube.

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:31.370
Look for container data center
Google, something like that.

00:30:31.370 --> 00:30:34.280
We have some videos
way back from 2005.

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:39.500
And it turned out to be a
great idea in some aspects.

00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:44.010
It was actually very efficient.

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:45.300
And it was pretty cheap.

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:47.300
But it turned out those
containers were actually

00:30:47.300 --> 00:30:48.420
sort of unwieldy.

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:50.090
You needed large
buildings to kind

00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:52.400
of maneuver them in
and out and a crane

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:54.260
and all kinds of things.

00:30:54.260 --> 00:30:57.890
And then, also, it would
really constrain your space.

00:30:57.890 --> 00:31:00.350
The shipping containers
is a fixed form factor.

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:02.300
You have to kind of
cram in your servers.

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:05.580
And they were not really
deep enough, et cetera.

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:09.920
And so really that was a
big failure in some extent.

00:31:09.920 --> 00:31:12.090
We did it once and never again.

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:14.390
But at the same
time, it was really

00:31:14.390 --> 00:31:15.890
one of these classic
opportunities

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:19.580
where it was fairly
fast to fail.

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:20.810
And we learned a ton.

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:23.210
And we actually used a
lot of those principles

00:31:23.210 --> 00:31:26.210
in our next data center,
which we actually

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:30.020
called containerless,
or the nontainer--

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:31.850
like the non container--

00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:36.620
where we could preserve a
lot of the cooling efficiency

00:31:36.620 --> 00:31:41.270
and the cheapness but,
basically, put all of that

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:46.040
into the row of the server,
versus an entire enclosure.

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:48.500
And so today, even
today, what you

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:52.760
see sort of in each server row
actually looks pretty low tech.

00:31:52.760 --> 00:31:54.890
If you go look up some
pictures or some videos,

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:56.090
it's just kind of metal.

00:31:56.090 --> 00:32:00.290
And the metal is really sort of
a mini container for a server

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:01.700
that's in each slot.

00:32:01.700 --> 00:32:03.740
But we don't have
the container around.

00:32:03.740 --> 00:32:06.200
So that is probably one
of my favorite failures,

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:08.030
because it definitely
was a failure.

00:32:08.030 --> 00:32:11.070
That was not, in the
end, a great idea.

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:14.090
But it led to, actually,
rapid innovation

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:15.860
in the data center
space and actually

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:22.465
was a step that although
you partially regret it,

00:32:22.465 --> 00:32:26.010
it was a failure
that really paid off.

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:27.090
AJA HAMMERLY: Yeah.

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:29.650
I remember reading a lot about
Google's containerized data

00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:30.150
centers.

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:33.120
And those were cool.

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:35.160
And I'm glad that lots
of cool technology

00:32:35.160 --> 00:32:36.870
came out of it too
and new approaches.

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:37.990
URS HOLZLE: They
were actually hot.

00:32:37.990 --> 00:32:39.550
They were actually
hot, 27 degrees.

00:32:39.550 --> 00:32:40.717
AJA HAMMERLY: They were hot.

00:32:40.717 --> 00:32:41.730
Yes, of course.

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:44.700
URS HOLZLE: 80 degrees
Fahrenheit, yes.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:46.680
AJA HAMMERLY: Google's
all about containers.

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:49.180
So last question.

00:32:49.180 --> 00:32:51.942
What will the next game
changer within cloud be?

00:32:51.942 --> 00:32:53.442
Is it going to be
quantum computing?

00:32:58.530 --> 00:33:01.290
URS HOLZLE: Probably
not the next one,

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:03.930
because quantum computing
is one of these things

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:07.620
that when it works,
it will be awesome.

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:11.220
But at the same time, there's
some really pretty big problems

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:11.730
left.

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:15.060
And though we're kind
of making progress,

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:17.880
it's always kind of
five years plus out.

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:20.280
And when someone tells
you it's five years out,

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:24.150
they really kind of mean, I'm
not sure how long it will take.

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:28.440
So I hope that there's
innovation in cloud before.

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:31.980
I do think that one
of the game changers

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:38.730
really is to change cloud away
from thinking about it as three

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:42.180
different clouds, and you have
to choose one of the three.

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:45.880
I think that really is going
to be outdated very soon.

00:33:45.880 --> 00:33:47.910
It's going to kind
feel like, for those

00:33:47.910 --> 00:33:51.660
that were around in 1995--

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:54.180
in 1995, it was
sort of-- servers

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:58.620
were new, away from mini
computers and mainframes.

00:33:58.620 --> 00:34:01.080
PC's were new, x86.

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:02.400
Unix was new.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:04.530
And Java was new.

00:34:04.530 --> 00:34:06.090
The enterprise
really kind of needed

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:07.950
to change how it did computing.

00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:10.949
And back then everyone
sort of obsessed about,

00:34:10.949 --> 00:34:14.909
should I pick DEC
or Sun or Compaq.

00:34:14.909 --> 00:34:17.293
Which vendor do I pick,
which hardware vendor?

00:34:17.293 --> 00:34:18.960
But it was not just
the hardware vendor.

00:34:18.960 --> 00:34:20.940
Because if you pick
one hardware vendor,

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:22.650
you've got a different OS.

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.150
And you've got a
different compiler.

00:34:24.150 --> 00:34:25.650
And you've got a
different database.

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:26.860
And you pick the whole thing.

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:28.710
Everything was different.

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:32.010
And that's kind of the mode
that we're in today with cloud.

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:33.929
If you pick one
cloud, then the way

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:35.520
you start the
container or the way

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:38.219
you start a VM is different
from somewhere else.

00:34:38.219 --> 00:34:40.407
But that actually kind
of doesn't make sense.

00:34:40.407 --> 00:34:41.699
It's just starting a container.

00:34:41.699 --> 00:34:43.866
And in the end sort of
everyone does the same thing.

00:34:43.866 --> 00:34:44.949
So why isn't it the same?

00:34:44.949 --> 00:34:48.150
So in the server
space back in '95,

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:51.120
it took about 10 years
for that to play out.

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:52.679
And 10 years later
people realized

00:34:52.679 --> 00:34:55.980
that it's not about
picking the server vendor.

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:57.880
That's important for
hardware reasons.

00:34:57.880 --> 00:35:00.240
But it's not a choice
that you have to stick

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:02.160
with for the next 20 years.

00:35:02.160 --> 00:35:05.970
The choice that
matters, back in 2005,

00:35:05.970 --> 00:35:09.060
was Linux versus Windows Server.

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:11.640
If you picked one of
them, that stayed with you

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:13.050
for a decade or two.

00:35:13.050 --> 00:35:15.932
And in this particular
case, if you picked Linux

00:35:15.932 --> 00:35:17.640
and if you picked open
source, you really

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:20.040
picked a growing
ecosystem around it.

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:22.110
And you could pick
anything below and pretty

00:35:22.110 --> 00:35:23.587
much anything above.

00:35:23.587 --> 00:35:25.170
And so that's what
we're really trying

00:35:25.170 --> 00:35:29.210
to do to the cloud space
with Anthos to say,

00:35:29.210 --> 00:35:31.140
Anthos is kind of
like [INAUDIBLE]..

00:35:31.140 --> 00:35:33.100
It's not the same
because it's managed.

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:34.200
So you do get open source.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:36.617
But you don't have to worry
about the updates and security

00:35:36.617 --> 00:35:37.680
patches, et cetera.

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:39.270
But it actually
has the same sort

00:35:39.270 --> 00:35:42.450
of horizontal property,
where now you don't really

00:35:42.450 --> 00:35:43.890
care about what's below.

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:46.480
You may still care about it for
performance or cost reasons.

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:48.270
But it's not a
stack that's below.

00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:49.810
It's kind of more like hosting.

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:52.570
And an above it's
pretty much anything.

00:35:52.570 --> 00:35:55.380
So any service you
want to consume,

00:35:55.380 --> 00:36:00.780
any development practice you
want to use, you can do that.

00:36:00.780 --> 00:36:02.650
And that's independent
of the environment.

00:36:02.650 --> 00:36:05.460
So that, to me, really
actually is a game changer,

00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:08.220
because it moves the
whole thinking away

00:36:08.220 --> 00:36:11.970
from sort of the
AOL model, pick AOL,

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:15.540
and then everything is the
AOL way, to the internet model

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:17.490
where you pick open standards.

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:20.160
And then you can mix and
match and really adopt

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:23.580
the things that work best
for you and do something.

00:36:23.580 --> 00:36:26.370
If you picked Linux 20 years
ago, it's still with you.

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:28.890
And it's still a great choice
because all the other things

00:36:28.890 --> 00:36:30.200
around it--

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:33.420
you could change your
opinion, your choice.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:36.070
And so Linux didn't
constrain you in that way.

00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:39.030
And that's really, I think,
what the big revolution in cloud

00:36:39.030 --> 00:36:43.500
will be, that Anthos
together with Kubernetes

00:36:43.500 --> 00:36:47.280
gives you that same portability
and that same learning at once.

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:50.040
And then just do it
for the next 10 years.

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:53.363
That really changes how
people look at cloud.

00:36:53.363 --> 00:36:55.030
AJA HAMMERLY: That
makes a lot of sense.

00:36:55.030 --> 00:36:58.163
And it totally makes sense that
moving toward open standards

00:36:58.163 --> 00:37:00.580
that work anywhere is going
to give people the flexibility

00:37:00.580 --> 00:37:03.830
to do what's right for
them, which is awesome.

00:37:03.830 --> 00:37:05.800
So I want to thank you
so much for chatting

00:37:05.800 --> 00:37:08.008
with us today and sharing
your thoughts and insights.

00:37:08.008 --> 00:37:10.060
And I want to thank our
audience for joining us

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:12.250
